  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\r\n\r\nGiven the following markup (from a WPT test):\r\n```html\r\n<svg><template><foreignObject><div></template>\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhat happens:\r\n1. [initial] `StartTag \"svg\"`           -> Handle normally\r\n2. [in body] `StartTag \"template\"`       -> Handle as foreign content (because SVG)\r\n3. [in body] `StartTag \"foreignobject\"`  -> Handle as foreign content\r\n4. [in body] `StartTag \"div\"`            -> Handle normally (because HTML integration point)\r\n5. [in body] `EndTag \"template\"`         -> Handle normally\r\n\r\nA problem occurs here when processing the `EndTag \"template\"` in (5). We end up in the rules for the \"in head\" insertion mode, where it's expected that we've pushed an insertion mode onto the stack of template insertion modes.\r\n\r\nHowever, because the `StartTag \"template\"` was handled as foreign content, we never pushed anything onto the stack of template insertion nodes (as the rules for foreign content don't say anything about `\"template\"`).\r\n\r\nAm I missing something, or is this indeed a bug in the spec?",
